Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  25 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON  25 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 11 45 CLINICAL DATA  Bilateral breast lesions                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS      SUPERIOR MEDIAL  LEFT BREAST   BIOPSY  1  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU AND ATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  SEE NOTE  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH MICROCYSTS  FIBROSIS  APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS  NOTE  Carcinoma in situ involves inked surgical margin   LOWER LESION OF LEFT BREAST   BIOPSY  1  ATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH MICROCYSTS  FIBROSIS  APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS  BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  1  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  60    POORLY DIFFERENTIATED GRADE REMOVED_DATE  AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  40    COMEDO TYPE  SEE NOTE  2  LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  NOTE  The infiltrating carcinoma extends to the inked margin in multiple places  Definitive vascular invasion is not identified  The invasive carcinoma measures 2 5cm in greatest dimensions  Frozen section diagnosis is confirmed  Diagnosis by  Christophe Girardet  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 15                                                                                     S PHASE ANALYSIS Addendum     BREAST   SUPERIOR MEDIAL LEFT  BIOPSY   BRDU UPTAKE  PERCENTAGE OF CELLS IN S PHASE   6   SEE NOTE  NOTE  The breast tumor tissue  after being incubated in medium containing a thymidine analog  bromodeoxyuridine  was processed and stained by immunohistochemical methods to detect those cells which were replicating DNA  Positive cells  cells in S phase  were counted by microscopic visualization of the breast tissue  In this case  the predominant lesion present in the tumor submitted for BRDU uptake was in situ carcinoma  Results from 372 MGH cases of invasive ductal carcinoma have shown cells in S phase ranging from 0  to 40   with a mean 6 3  and median of 5  Benign lesions  and invasive lobular carcinomas have consistantly shown very low counts  less than 3    Added by  Ann D  Thor  M D  Signed On  02 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 28     HORMONE BINDING ASSAY Addendum     RESULTS OF HORMONE BINDING ASSAYS AS REPORTED BY DAVID T  MACLAUGHLIN  PhD   726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   estrogen receptor  Negative  progesterone receptor  Negative  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  13 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10 43     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FROZEN TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   The tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  the results of prospective staining for estrogen receptor protein of 100 unfixed breast tumors were compared with determinations of estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  93  of tumors that reacted with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  100  of tumors that failed to stain were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining for progesterone receptor protein of 50 breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Seventy three percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 91  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  27 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10 12 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in two parts  each  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number  Labeled  Superior medial lesion  left breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm piece of fibrofatty tissue with a needle localization wire and accompanying specimen x ray  The surface of the specimen is inked  Sectioning reveals white  fibrous parenchyma with fatty streaks and a small  firm  gray area  0 4 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm   that abuts an inked surface  The entire specimen is submitted in cassettes A through E  Labeled  Lower lesion  left breast  is a small piece of fibrofatty tissue with a needle localization wire and accompanying specimen x ray showing microcalcifications  The specimen measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm  The external surface is inked and sectioned  revealing a white  fibrous  shiny tissue with fatty streaks  No areas are suspicious for infiltrating carcinoma  No frozen section is performed  The specimen is submitted entirely in cassettes F through J  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number and  Right breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm firm  nodular piece of fibrofatty tissue  The specimen is inked and sectioned  revealing a firm  gritty  irregular mass measuring 2 5cm in diameter  The mass abuts an inked margin  A representative section is frozen as Frozen Section  1 and the block is kept frozen  In addition  a representative section is submitted for estrogen receptors and BRDU studies  Representative sections are submitted in cassettes K through R  Resident  C  Diana Jordan  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS GROSS  1  SUPERIOR MEDIAL LESION OF LEFT BREAST 1 OF 2   NO AREAS GROSSLY SUSPICIOUS FOR CARCINOMA  FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS GROSS  2  LOWER LESION OF LEFT BREAST   NO AREAS GROSSLY SUSPICIOUS FOR CARCINOMA  FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  3  BREAST RIGHT   INFILTRATING AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  Jo Ann E  Haney  MD O R  Diagnosis by  John Andrew Carlson  MD O R  Staff Pathologist  Eugene Jerome Mark  M D                                                                